# Kotlin Gray-Part — Per-Project Fingerprint

Stores scan for structural patterns shared across submissions from the same
developer. Folder names, class names, codec seed, `259200`, `Chrome/131…`,
`__flsa`, `KbPan`, `fl_state` — every one of these is a value that survives
casual renaming and clusters a portfolio. Two apps sharing enough of them
can get **all related accounts banned in a chain**.

The whole point of this template is that this class of mistake **is not
possible to make by hand any more**. Two mechanisms take care of it.

---

## 1. Automated derivation from `gray.seed`

`app/build.gradle.kts` reads `gray.properties`, feeds `gray.seed` into a
deterministic RNG, and emits into `BuildConfig` a value for every one of
the following. The Kotlin code reads only from `BuildConfig`, so the source
tree carries **none** of these as literals:

- SharedPreferences plain + encrypted file names
- Every preference key inside them (`run_ch`, `dest_u`, …)
- JS safe-area sentinel, JS keyboard sentinel, JS bridge class name
- FCM channel id + human-facing title
- Push snooze seconds (2–7 days range), organic GCD delay, config timeout,
  attribution first-launch / return timeouts, deep-link wait, GCD timeout,
  connectivity grace, safe-area re-inject delay, heartbeat interval,
  redirect retry budget
- Chrome major / build / patch numbers in the User-Agent
- XOR codec seed byte array (24–40 random bytes), multiplier, offset, and
  algorithm variant (three interchangeable shapes rotate across projects)
- Encoded config endpoint / AppsFlyer key / Firebase project number / GCD
  base — encoded at build time with the derived codec

To rotate any of it: change `gray.seed` (`gradlew graySeed` produces a fresh
one) and rebuild. Do **not** hand-edit any of the derived files.

---

## 2. Automated code-shape rotation with `tools/rebrand.py`

Package layout and class names cannot be encoded into `BuildConfig` — R8
does mangle them, but a stack trace or `dexdump` still reveals them.
`tools/rebrand.py` handles the rotation:

- Application package (`com.example.grayshell` → `com.<project>.<word>`)
- Kotlin sub-package folder names — chosen from the seed against a theme
  word bank in the script
- Every class name in the gray flow (Application, router, WebView host,
  keyboard, offline, permission, config client, attribution, FCM,
  storage, connectivity, blueprint, screens)
- Drawable resource prefix (`gray_*` → theme-picked)

The tool prints its plan first; nothing changes on disk until `--apply`.
Add themes freely — the pool of word banks is easy to widen.

---

## 3. Mandatory rules

1. **Never share `gray.seed` across submissions.** Not even between debug
   forks of the same team — a leaked debug build with a shared seed
   clusters just as loudly. Generate a fresh one for every project with
   `gradlew graySeed`.
2. **Never commit `gray.properties`.** It's in `.gitignore`; keep it there.
   The seed and credentials both live in it.
3. **Never hand-edit `BuildConfig` fields, encoded arrays, storage keys,
   sentinels, or JS bridge names.** They are derived. If you find yourself
   writing "259200" or "__flsa" in code, you are undoing the whole
   mechanism.
4. **Run `tools/rebrand.py` for every new project.** Skipping it ships two
   apps with identical class names and identical folder paths — the same
   cluster join the encoded arrays were meant to break.
5. **Rotate library versions per project.** Stagger `okhttp`, `AppsFlyer
   SDK`, `Firebase BOM`, `androidx.security`, coroutines — the dependency
   set is itself a fingerprint. `app/build.gradle.kts` is a normal Gradle
   file; edit the versions there.
6. **Vary the notification icon shape.** Reuse the framework but redraw
   the glyph; a shared monochrome shape is a visible cluster join.

---

## 4. Verification — what to grep for

Before every submission run these — every one must return **no** matches
inside `app/src/main/java/`. `gradlew grayReport` prints the derived
values in one place for eyeballing.

```
rg -n '259_?200|Chrome/131|__flsa\b|__kb\b|KbPan|fl_state|fl_enc|app_push_channel|ChangeThisSeed'
rg -n 'com\.example\.grayshell'
rg -n 'Log\.i\(|Log\.w\(|Log\.e\('    # only inside core/Trace.kt is allowed
```

If a match falls outside `core/Trace.kt` for the last line, the log call
is not guarded by `BuildConfig.DEBUG` and will leak into a release build.
Route it through `Trace`.

---

## 5. Do NOT hide (breaks functionality)

| Item | Why it must stay visible |
|------|--------------------------|
| `INTERNET`, `ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE` perms | no network otherwise |
| `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` perm | API 33+ system dialog needs it |
| FCM service + channel meta-data | pushes silently dropped |
| `adjustResize` on the shell activity | keyboard covers inputs |
| `google-services.json` | Firebase init fails |
| App Check activate | config requests rejected (403) |
| `networkSecurityConfig` cleartext | http partner content blocked |

---

## 6. Release hygiene

- `debugForceStreamUrl` is empty in release under all circumstances; the
  build script forces it.
- No `applicationIdSuffix` (breaks attribution — pitfalls #1).
- R8 + resource shrinking on in release. `proguard-rules.pro` keeps
  Firebase / AppsFlyer / OkHttp / WebView JS-interface / entry classes.
- `Trace` compiles out completely — verify with `BuildConfig.DEBUG == false`
  producing dead branches in the release Kotlin.
- Keystore + `keystore.properties` are gitignored; never commit them.
